## Creating Components

ReactJS is built around the concept of components. A component is a reusable piece of code that can be used to create a user interface. To create a component, you need to define a class that extends the React.Component class. Here is an example of a simple component:

```javascript
import React, { Component } from 'react';

class MyComponent extends Component {
    render() {
        return <div>Hello World!</div>;
    }
}
```

In the above example, we have created a component called MyComponent. The render function returns the HTML to be displayed by the component. In this case, it displays a simple message.

## Using Components

Once you have created a component, you can use it in your application by importing it and adding it to the DOM. Here is an example of how to use the MyComponent component we created earlier:

```javascript
import MyComponent from './MyComponent';

class App extends Component {
    render() {
        return <MyComponent />;
    }
}
```

In the above example, we have imported the MyComponent component and added it to the DOM by rendering it inside the App component.

## Updating Components

ReactJS is a reactive library, which means that it automatically updates the UI whenever the state of the component changes. To update a component, you need to call the setState method with the new state. Here is an example of how to update a component:

```javascript
import React, { Component } from 'react';

class MyComponent extends Component {
    constructor(props) {
        super(props);
        this.state = { count: 0 };
    }

    incrementCount() {
        this.setState({ count: this.state.count + 1 });
    }

    render() {
        return (
            <div>
                <p>Count: {this.state.count}</p>
                <button onClick={() => this.incrementCount()}>Increment</button>
            </div>
        );
    }
}
```

